{{smaller block|''Trayle'' or trayne of a clothe.
{{right|''Prompt. Parv.'', p. 499.}}}}

{{smaller|(''b'') A trailing part or organ; a train: as, the ''trail'' of the peacock: often used figuratively.}}

{{smaller block|A sudden star, it shot through liquid air,
And drew behind a radiant ''trail'' of hair.
{{right|''Pope'', R. of the L., v. 128.}}}}

{{smaller block|It is no easy matter to picture to ourselves the blazing ''trail'' of splendour which in such a pageant [the coronation of Anne Boleyn] must have drawn along the London streets.
{{right|''Froude'', Sketches, p. 175.}}}}

{{smaller|(''c'') In ''artillery'', the lower end of the carriage; in field-artillery, that part of the carriage which rests on the}}

